 \subsection{Outstanding MCR Evidence}
 \paragraph{MCR decision and next review.} The MCR defines a conditionally feasible spacecraft concept with a maximum design mass of 3~kg. The detailed payload-electronics, data, ADCS control, power-distribution and RF interfaces are PDR design products. PDR entry requires traceable subsystem requirements, reconciled BOM, validation of the updated 8--10-year drag-to-cutoff study and a separate disposal assessment and measured or source-backed margins.
+
+\paragraph{New evidence disposition.}
+The corrected three-scenario evidence establishes complete named timestamp spans and reconciled native contact/data totals, superseding the old truncation finding. Runtime source/configuration and transition/slew semantics remain unresolved. Independent thermal component limits and detumble model/censoring records are still required. Current detector response, optics/electronics, actuator qualification, structural/thermal tests and RF net throughput remain open; no science-tier threshold changes.
+
+\paragraph{Preliminary science-data handling.}
+The 292-byte v3 record and three-reading preference remain unchanged. The corrected annual legacy-gate EMSC sensitivity generates 46637364 bytes, transfers that volume at full modeled 19.2 kbps, and peaks at 75336 bytes queued. This supports conditional data-handling feasibility for the sampled workload. A worst-start seven-day outage raises the required queue to 1010028 bytes: a 256 KiB RAM-only queue is insufficient for that case; nonvolatile spill is required. The 128 MiB candidate partition exceeds this modeled backlog before reserves, but sustained current-FPDU workload and retention remain unqualified. The separate geometric-grid archive is not mixed into this time-aligned annual run. Unknown logger/configuration identity and actual science response prevent unconditional mission closure.
